.TH GOBAN-DAEMON 8 "August 2026" "goban" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
goban-daemon \- supervised log watcher and confirmed kernel IP banner
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B goban-daemon
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B goban-daemon
watches file, Docker, or systemd-journal sources for authentication and
application abuse. Rules extract an IP address with a named regular-expression
capture, count strikes in a sliding time window, and apply a timeout-bearing
kernel ban through iptables+ipset or native nftables.
.PP
A ban is counted, audited, and removed from strike state only after the
firewall backend confirms it. Source lifecycle, reconnects, queue drops, and
firewall-list errors are exposed through
.BR goban-client (1).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-config " " \fIPATH\fR
Path to the strict YAML configuration file (default
.IR /etc/goban/goban.yaml ).
.TP
.BR \-rules-dir " " \fIDIR\fR
Override the configured enabled-rule directory. Every *.yaml and *.yml file
is decoded strictly and merged at startup/reload.
.TP
.BR \-version
Print version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h " | " \-help
Print usage and exit.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/goban/goban.yaml
Main configuration.
.TP
.I /etc/goban/rules.d/
Enabled rule bundles. Packages install only stock-safe bundles here.
.TP
.I /usr/share/goban/rules-available/
Complete bundled rule library. Copy a file into rules.d only after defining
all sources it references.
.TP
.I /var/lib/goban/state-<rule>.gob
Per-rule fingerprinted strike state. Incompatible rule semantics cause old
state to be discarded rather than loaded into a changed rule.
.TP
.I /var/lib/goban/state-bans.json
Ban attribution metadata used to retain rule/source context across daemon
restarts. Kernel state remains authoritative for whether a ban is active.
.TP
.I /var/log/goban/audit.log
JSON-lines timeline of confirmed automatic/manual bans and successful unbans.
The bundled recidive rule consumes confirmed ban events.
.TP
.I /run/goban/goban.sock
Unix control socket. Mode and group are configured with socket_mode and
socket_group; an unknown group fails socket startup.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGTERM " | " SIGINT
Graceful shutdown. Strike and attribution state are saved. Kernel sets are
preserved unless flush_on_exit is enabled.
.TP
.B SIGHUP
Transactional hot reload. GoBan strictly validates and builds a complete
candidate graph, starts candidate sources, reconciles bounded old/new queues,
and swaps only after successful startup. On candidate failure, the active
graph is resumed unchanged. Equivalent to
.BR "goban-client reload" .
.SH CONFIGURATION SAFETY
Unknown YAML fields are rejected. Rule and source identifiers are restricted
to a safe character set. Every rule requires a named ip capture; datepattern
requires a named time capture; exclusion and trusted-proxy capture names must
exist in the regex. Unsafe zero-width allowlists and kernel-invalid ban
durations are refused.
.PP
Rules, sources, defaults, and the global allowlist support hot reload.
Firewall, socket, logging, audit, and persistence settings require restart;
a reload that changes them is refused.
.SH SOURCE HEALTH
Sources have starting, running, degraded, and stopped states. File sources use
a bounded rotation/truncation-aware follower. Docker sources reconnect event
and log streams and periodically reconcile matching containers. Journald
builds apply an sdjournal data threshold. Fan-out queues are bounded and
non-blocking; drops are counted rather than hidden.
.SH BACKENDS
.TP
.B iptables
Default. Creates timeout-capable IPv4/IPv6 ipsets, installs set-match drop
rules in each configured banner.iptables_chains entry (INPUT and FORWARD by
default), and uses direct ipset netlink for runtime ban/list/unban operations.
Re-banning refreshes the kernel TTL.
.TP
.B nftables
Uses nf_tables netlink directly. Creates one inet table, IPv4/IPv6 timeout
sets, an input-hook chain, and an optional forward-hook chain.
.PP
Forward protection is required for normal bridged/routed container traffic.
Hosts with a custom firewall manager should choose and test the appropriate
FORWARD, DOCKER-USER, or dedicated chain explicitly.
.SH DATE HANDLING
Traditional syslog timestamps receive the nearest plausible year and are
parsed in the configured timezone. A date-aware rule defaults to fail-closed:
malformed or implausibly stale/future timestamps are dropped. The optional
source_time policy explicitly permits source-event-time fallback.
.SH REVERSE PROXIES
A rule that extracts a forwarded client IP should also configure
trusted_proxy_capture and trusted_proxies. The forwarded IP is rejected unless
the captured direct peer belongs to an allowed proxy CIDR.
.SH PERSISTENCE
Strike state does not recreate kernel bans. Kernel sets survive a daemon
restart but are normally lost at host reboot. The optional goban-persist.service
saves only GoBan's default ipsets and default nftables table; custom names need
a unit override.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Clean shutdown.
.TP
.B 1
Startup/runtime failure.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Selected GOBAN_* variables overlay YAML before rule-directory loading and
final default application. See README.md for the supported list.
.SH CAPABILITIES
The daemon requires CAP_NET_ADMIN. Docker log sources additionally require
access to the Docker socket. Journald support requires a journald-tagged build.
The shipped systemd unit bounds capabilities and applies filesystem and kernel
hardening.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR goban-client (1),
.BR iptables (8),
.BR ipset (8),
.BR nft (8)
